#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084017} :activity/id #uuid "f95be382-38ca-5542-bef3-419ba331df88" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/id #uuid "19f4470c-27bd-57f0-bd24-c70403f1614f" :topic/math-academy-id 725} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084017 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084422 :tutorial/content "A function is said to be an **even function** if it is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\nFor example, the function \n\n$$\nf(x) = x^2,\n$$\n\nwhose graph is depicted below, is an example of an even function. It is symmetric about the $y$-axis, meaning that if we reflect the graph over the $y$-axis, we get the same picture.\n\n![](images/cf/cfca345c7ca094cacdbc6d7141a09e34fd116c677629a6a4c1917fb0db1f753f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "263b7003-1ee6-55aa-94cd-5ee25cbf63a4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2774 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2819e00a-c45a-50f1-a7ea-e3637612c182" :step/math-academy-id 14086 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084049}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084018 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083166 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082983 :question/id #uuid "66135849-b0f8-53a9-aab8-4b6aebde3cd9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3994"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d887d9f-b9c4-54b3-a53e-691989bda962" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048474}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083674} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519604} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563677}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "c9f1a487-6342-5b97-86bc-81ffa4a8d287" :step/math-academy-id 14088 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084423 :tutorial/content "Recall that the graph of the even function $f(x) = x^2$ is symmetric about the $y$-axis.\n\n![](images/07/07d0699c46b3cbd215c461d5df3629d86cd31a93228d6790fdc65b383e8556fc.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1=f(1) \\\\ f(-2) & =4=f(2) \\\\ f(-3) & =9=f(3) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, both $x$ and $-x$ give the same value of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as follows: $f(-x) = f(x).$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **even** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "4c7a0bb9-4765-505f-a538-dd4a1426f8c3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2776 :tutorial/title "The Algebraic Definition of Even Functions"} :step/id #uuid "95f32820-f0d3-5c90-856a-b03f017bb399" :step/math-academy-id 14090 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084020 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083167 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082984 :question/id #uuid "5b22df97-b731-5d77-a6ed-2a90efcd95a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3996"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8bff4063-9e51-53fa-ab55-e68540e6f587" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083670} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083675} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519544} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563681}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating an Expression Given the Table for an Even or Odd Function"} :step/id #uuid "6f3a0616-90b9-5a24-8eb2-82152eed463a" :step/math-academy-id 14091} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084049 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082992 :question/id #uuid "f8d678e6-c0db-534d-9ae2-0551ea8201bb" :question/math-academy-id "e-4172"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eb0d0f84-a628-5605-a75f-f4f5459e047c" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050151}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519556} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519580}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even Functions Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "8c6a848e-062e-550a-98cc-a5b33fc8efb8" :step/math-academy-id 15131 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084050}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084050 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084430 :tutorial/content "A function is said to be an **odd function** if it has **rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin**. That is to say, if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\nFor example, the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=x,\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below, is an example of an odd function. It has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin, meaning that if we rotate the graph of an odd function by $180^\\circ$ about the origin, we end up with the same picture.\n\n![](images/ff/ff49c826d9a6a36b35edf73689d30e5d9dadc976b5f1caf47c1dd0383f9893bc.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1184d837-2701-5c4d-806c-6af72dee583b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2840 :tutorial/title "Classifying Odd Functions Graphically"} :step/id #uuid "e25c1d87-8e4c-58bc-98db-694f2852ceb3" :step/math-academy-id 15132 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084018}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084431 :tutorial/content "Now, recall that the graph of the odd function $g(x)=x$ has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n![](images/a0/a0dd6a5cbc09182a4a87569879a97da697a21eeda869ae20f512dc86a911b75e.png)\n\nNotice the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-1=-f(1) \\\\ f(-2) & =-2=-f(2) \\\\ f(-3) & =-3=-f(3) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for every $x$ in the domain, $x$ and $-x$ give opposite values of the function. Algebraically, this fact can be written as $f(-x) = -f(x).$\n\nIn general, a function $f(x)$ is said to be **odd** if\n\n$$\nf(-x) = -f(x)\n$$\n\n \nfor each $x$ in the domain of the function." :tutorial/id #uuid "db19e6ba-49d7-534c-b84b-24dfd8866658" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2844 :tutorial/title "The Algebraic Definition of Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "46e959d5-c850-58b6-aabc-e6143b9e3a64" :step/math-academy-id 15142 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084052}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083185 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082993 :question/id #uuid "373bdf54-aaa4-5e94-82a7-95c0a396623a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4183"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4fa847d9-f0bf-57fb-92b2-27c8a7cdb3b1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083893} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083894} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234216} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563708}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Even and Odd Functions Using the Algebraic Definition"} :step/id #uuid "b60bbea3-5236-5bc1-93fe-54234cf0b4fb" :step/math-academy-id 15151 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186084020}}] :activity/title "Even and Odd Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186083420}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536044} :activity/id #uuid "f7531106-8081-593d-b221-3db6da6b1b3b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/id #uuid "19f4470c-27bd-57f0-bd24-c70403f1614f" :topic/math-academy-id 725} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536041 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c2efd2b-f00a-593f-bb81-c9dd053ed073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535626} {:answer/id #uuid "bb33137b-01b7-5b1b-99cc-1a5450ea9b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535723} {:answer/id #uuid "ca604a35-1898-54ea-be79-3d86d2c26139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535739} {:answer/id #uuid "da8a3e87-40ac-5983-bfbe-cf585598fdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535768} {:answer/id #uuid "e5951a84-f04c-5c6f-9a06-d5001b3dda61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535739} :answer-field/id #uuid "987364e2-83c5-563b-a86c-5c9e8d2fcffd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "71f2cfbf-31cb-5074-ba4b-cb4c0df22324" :question/math-academy-id "q-110665" :question/problem "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-90$ | $-65$ | $-40$ | $40$ | $65$ | $90$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $16$ | $b-2$ | $-8$ | $-8$ | $-1$ | $a+6$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $f(-x)=f(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $f(-90)=f(90),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-90) & =f(90) \\\\ 16 & =a+6 \\\\ a & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(-65)=f(65),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-65) & =f(65) \\\\ b-2 & =-1 \\\\ b & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=10+1=11.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b7a0c930-adde-574d-8760-a44307f3040c"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536044 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083894 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b087c98d-eb7c-5ce5-82c7-979c21323672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "It's not a function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082296} {:answer/id #uuid "d83acd8e-fca2-5275-988a-6b470f499acb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082297} {:answer/id #uuid "039f1f39-8490-577c-8657-6a91decc92dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Both even and odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082298} {:answer/id #uuid "5fa40858-bb4e-5eb9-98c9-f905f338e539" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Neither even nor odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082299} {:answer/id #uuid "5fdc40a8-1125-5cd9-87f6-7f51bdd2283e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082300}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082297} :answer-field/id #uuid "a88af808-6d41-5393-8859-9acb647cc2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5078528-4e53-5684-a818-c4b067e4dfab" :question/math-academy-id "q-6684" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$, for $x\\ne 0$, even, odd, or neither?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x,$ while\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x.$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =\\frac{1}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=f(x),$ the function is even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne -f(x),$ the function is not odd.\n\nTherefore, the function is only even."} :step/id #uuid "d955d2cc-d916-5f24-af15-9912f4a42329" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536046}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536046 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083675 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8655fcd4-4c8b-57b7-9496-b042577adad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080982} {:answer/id #uuid "078e8bdf-610e-5d6f-bec2-f28e3aa5ea64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080983} {:answer/id #uuid "0e699b69-a4eb-599a-bb71-823303466e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080984} {:answer/id #uuid "55b58e9d-e155-5967-9343-0ad0053d6a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080985} {:answer/id #uuid "68e3fdda-7135-59c5-acdf-a679759b65ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080986}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186080983} :answer-field/id #uuid "5abfecb6-3973-5d80-b497-dd0ac1014593" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8685c18f-3b3e-5a81-8a52-9c0ab8ac96f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-36592" :question/problem "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is an even function, find the value of $a+b.$\n\n| **$x$** | $-23$ | $-15$ | $-7$ | $7$ | $15$ | $23$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $a+5$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $b+1$ | $4$ | $-\\sqrt{2}$ | $6$ |" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x)$ is an even function, we must have that $f(-x)=f(x).$ \n\nUsing the fact that $f(-23)=f(23),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-23) & =f(23) \\\\ a+5 & =6 \\\\ a & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $f(-7)=f(7),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-7) & =f(7) \\\\ b+1 & =4 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b=1+3=4.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "dc6fbc89-df59-502f-a5d5-280777efba84" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536051}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536047 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536004 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bae23a-2b5c-5549-8d74-d4e7ed7715fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535492} {:answer/id #uuid "4c5799db-521f-5c06-9242-e01b2fe426b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535548} {:answer/id #uuid "6b569b29-0310-5803-aa5e-5c8b3097fb67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535602} {:answer/id #uuid "9a658323-9478-5cd3-bbe5-ee65e87c40c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535679} {:answer/id #uuid "a4de91ac-6edf-528c-8e95-7ba1adfbf5b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535691}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535679} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd416d50-8739-51d0-a2e4-b34b56fbb8eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535903}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f2ca34f0-80b3-509b-af1e-131cf84ebf9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-72703" :question/problem "![](images/d4/d4cf4f9e1cc145154e5f542a5064b51d6385e7f81d12c7187dff436f5e7e10f9.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs II and III do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, they are **not** odd functions.\n\n- On the other hand, graphs I and IV do have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graphs ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same pictures. This means that graphs I and IV show odd functions.\n\n![](images/60/60a38abf4723f137ef0f824613ab15282c9ff5e3098fac13173630baef577e45.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and IV only.\""} :step/id #uuid "ed0e6744-5277-5979-857b-90d5d86b76ba" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536041}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536051 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535936 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba8a60b-baa6-5af0-9686-d3d8e8a4a09b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Both even and odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535456} {:answer/id #uuid "781a4eaa-66ed-5f45-b66c-ceb488944744" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Neither Even nor Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535619} {:answer/id #uuid "790f62a9-5b85-5ad8-99aa-12b309ae3d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535621} {:answer/id #uuid "f31eab5d-3671-5f28-812a-1245b40e2a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "It's not a function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535808} {:answer/id #uuid "f9addbd9-40e1-582a-8e8e-69659487f294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535819}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535621} :answer-field/id #uuid "7744ee0a-5590-5bee-b223-157ef8ebf071" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1b73902c-ad8b-52f6-bdea-d56e526c20d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-72131" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x)=6x$ even, odd, or neither?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =6(-x) \\\\  & =-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even. \n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-(6x) \\\\  & =-6x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd."} :step/id #uuid "f9fbce21-df1a-5096-ad2f-9cba5ddbace4" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536047}}] :activity/title "Even and Odd Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535434}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563805} :activity/id #uuid "41678cc0-e593-570b-9d61-39b46d08abce" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873 :topic/id #uuid "19f4470c-27bd-57f0-bd24-c70403f1614f" :topic/math-academy-id 725} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563782 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b5c879-2bfc-5ae7-aa01-8fe08c59be91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562997} {:answer/id #uuid "284ae4e5-3a75-512a-96da-43be36b80b67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Both even and odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563036} {:answer/id #uuid "54a22cd0-e927-5fcf-9c2a-bab375f9af7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Neither even nor odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563137} {:answer/id #uuid "7dc2f9cc-543f-5ad1-a14a-7870a7b49c6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "It's not a function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563221} {:answer/id #uuid "f24d99a9-b905-5ab2-8163-7cffe9f00a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563487} :answer-field/id #uuid "94f1e14e-2d96-586a-93e5-1ee8371fd0d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "81602105-d969-550d-9191-b0d8d8e1aacf" :question/math-academy-id "q-72132" :question/problem "Is the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+8x$ even, odd, or neither?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$\n\nComputing $f(-x)$ for the given function and comparing it to $f(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-x) & =(-x)^{3}+8(-x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)\\ne f(x),$ the function is not even.\n\nLastly, we compute $-f(x)$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-f(x) & =-({x}^{3}+8x) \\\\  & =-{x}^{3}-8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(-x)=-f(x),$ the function is odd."} :step/id #uuid "545bc88a-4a8b-5887-ade6-fe79621432ec" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563816}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563681 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54507137-2751-518f-a7f2-bf1dc9ab044b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563136} {:answer/id #uuid "9df12679-6f98-598d-ba66-d0981661d244" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Even" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563298} {:answer/id #uuid "a55644e4-c45b-579a-9be2-0a59b0e03d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "It's not a function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563320} {:answer/id #uuid "b754548b-7d8c-5141-94fa-e1ffa626e2bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Neither even nor odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563348} {:answer/id #uuid "edf6e733-5e69-5efe-8ed4-57ef94ac17ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "Both even and odd" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563478}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563136} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb959f88-2ef8-53d8-991e-77e015bb77af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "488cdf32-d755-59bf-889e-a9cbc58dc41a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5870" :question/problem "The table below defines the function $f(x).$ Is the function even, odd, or neither?\n\n| **$x$** | $-6$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $85$ | $75$ | $30$ | $-30$ | $-75$ | $-85$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n- a function is even if $f(-x)=f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\n- a function is odd if $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of $f$\n\nFor our function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-6) & =85=-f(6), \\\\ f(-4) & =75=-f(4), \\\\ f(-2) & =30=-f(2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $f(-x)=-f(x)$ for all $x$ in the domain of the function. Therefore, $f(x)$ is an odd function.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ded6bb87-7c64-5310-bc38-a96687a81f7e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563782}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563816 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09408971-4d52-596c-8834-deedff8b5844" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562957} {:answer/id #uuid "58b55549-1f9a-54cb-ae86-f76e94971508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563142} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b0e609-1235-5fa1-becc-779f66d23762" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563322} {:answer/id #uuid "c7aedaf8-9103-5c9c-b19c-4b12dccbbfed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III and IV only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563384} {:answer/id #uuid "e14c93c6-83bb-5c7c-9828-cce9626951e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563443}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563443} :answer-field/id #uuid "050fe5b1-848b-5f4c-879f-6f713c662044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186563519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "422c65f7-5866-5757-b32d-736e922491f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-110654" :question/problem "![](images/58/58e4554f6e5081ec5db6876222a7eeea24135743e4b68e25204084e097066ea4.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graphs II, III, and IV do not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, it is **not** an odd function.\n\n- On the other hand, graph I does have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graph  I  show odd functions.\n\n![](images/3b/3b5e234a3416a5e7100468b81f170171a873508eb0c87bba126009daca9e49bc.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "fde45626-5d27-5ac1-aae7-f958ff15be66"}] :activity/title "Even and Odd Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186562927}]}
